// The regression guard for epic #1837's tracked gap: a collection forced from
// a thread that is a genuine, registered WORLD mutator but owns no SCHEDULER
// TASK at all must still have its own OS-thread stack scanned — ROOT CLASS 8b
// (#2650), keyed off `worldStackTop`, populated by `worldSetStackTop` from
// `mutatorSlot` (`runtime/gc/gcworldsync.bit:384`) right after a thread first
// claims its World slot.
//
// #2645 originally found this a genuine hole: `worldSetStackTop` had no
// caller anywhere in the tree (#2646 added the storage and the doors; #2649,
// which populates it, had not landed), so `stwScanOwnStack` ran on every
// unheld collection and scanned nothing — this program's payload, held only
// as a raw `int` on this thread's own stack, got swept (`FAIL observed=7`,
// reproduced across 5+ runs under both `BIT_GC` policies, mutation-proven by
// removing the collection call). #2649 and #3492 landed together at
// `03d15de3`: `mutatorSlot` now calls `worldSetStackTop(world, slot,
// threadStackTop())` on the claim, and every `stwPollOn` call this thread
// makes reaches that claim through `worldEnsureSelfOn` before it ever tries to
// collect — so by the time this program forces its first collection, its own
// slot's stack top is already published. Re-run against merged `main`: 10/10
// runs `PASS` under both policies (this file's own `.expected`). The hole is
// closed for this path; this program now stands as the guard that would catch
// it reopening.
//
// #2651 adds a second, narrower assertion alongside the payload check: that
// `stw.bit`'s `bit_rt_port_stw_own_scans` counter — incremented only when
// `stwScanOwnStack` actually scans a range, not merely attempts one — stays
// equal to `bit_rt_port_stw_unheld` for this thread's own collections. The
// payload surviving proves class 8b covered THIS ONE object; the counter
// equality proves it covered EVERY unheld collection this run made, not just
// the lucky one, and would catch a regression the payload check alone could
// miss (e.g. a future change that scans this thread's stack but stops
// counting it, or starts refusing on some collections and not others).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY A RAW OS THREAD, NOT `spawn`
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `spawn` creates a GREEN TASK, which the scheduler registers in
// `runtime/sched`'s task registry — exactly the mapping classes 4/8/10 use to
// find a stack's bounds. That would hide the root class this file exists to
// exercise. This program instead calls `runtime/thread/darwin`'s
// `threadStart` directly (the same door `_tests_/stress/gcthreadsdarwin` and
// `_tests_/stress/thread/gcprops` use): the resulting thread has no green task,
// is never in `schedRegHead`'s list, and — per `runtime/thread/thread.bit`'s
// own module doc — is registered as a WORLD mutator lazily, on its first
// allocation or safepoint, entirely independently of the scheduler.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THE PAYLOAD IS A RAW `int`, NEVER A TYPED REFERENCE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `gcAlloc` already returns a plain `int` address, not a managed reference —
// there is no typed handle to accidentally keep here. The local that carries
// it (`raw`, below) is read by nothing else in its own frame after the
// collection, so the compiler's own stack map — which only ever names
// reference-typed slots — has no entry for it regardless of the classes under
// test. What actually stands between it and the sweep is whichever
// CONSERVATIVE class (4, 8, 10, 8b) covers the thread's stack; this program
// arranges for none of them to.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY A PRIVATE COLLECTOR (`_tests_/stress/stwcollect`'s technique), NOT AMBIENT
// `BIT_GC=stress`
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The stress harness (`_tests_/bit/stress`) runs every program TWICE and
// requires BYTE-IDENTICAL stdout both times — once under the default policy,
// once under `BIT_GC=stress`. A program that relies on the ambient policy to
// decide whether it collects cannot satisfy that: under the default policy a
// handful of small allocations may never cross the real heap trigger. So this
// program drives its OWN collector instance through `stwPollOn` — never bound
// through `stwBind`/`worldBind`, so the live scheduler and the live collector
// never see it (see `stwPollOn`'s own comment on why that is safe) —
// configured to collect UNCONDITIONALLY on every poll. That makes the
// exposure deterministic regardless of which policy the harness runs this
// binary under. Driving it from the RAW THREAD created above, rather than
// from `main`, is what keeps "no registered task owns this stack" genuine
// instead of simulated: the private scheduler block below is left entirely
// empty (no fake task is ever registered in it), which is exactly the state
// a real worker between tasks, or this thread, is in.
//
// `runtime/stw/stw.bit`'s exported `bit_rt_port_stw_scans` /
// `bit_rt_port_stw_unheld` counters are process-wide, incremented by
// `stwScanTasks` on EVERY call regardless of which World/sched pair drove it
// — so reading them here after the join proves this thread's own collection
// really went through that path.
//
// PRINTED AS DERIVED BOOLEANS, NOT RAW COUNTS — same reason
// `_tests_/stress/stwcollect` prints `ran=${gcStatCollections(g) > 0}` rather
// than the raw count: the exact numbers are not reproducible between the two
// policy runs (this thread's ordinary loop back-edges also poll the REAL
// ambient collector, and how many of those actually collect depends on the
// active policy), but "at least one of this thread's own collections was
// unheld" is true either way once the forced poll below has run.
//
// Constraints from the ticket and epic #1837, honored explicitly: no
// `spawn`; the payload is never held as a typed reference anywhere on this
// thread; no file under `runtime/` is touched; every loop below is bounded by
// a literal constant.

import { threadStart, threadRelease } from "../../../runtime/thread/darwin"
import { threadJoin, threadJoinDone, threadRunning } from "../../../runtime/thread"
import { parkSleepNs, parkMonoNs } from "../../../runtime/park/darwin"
import { byteAt } from "../thread/props"
import { spinUnlocked } from "../../../runtime"
import { heapInit, heapWords } from "../../../runtime/alloc"
import { gcInit, gcConfigure, gcAlloc, gcOwns } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { gcWords, gcBindMemory, storeWord, loadWord, reserveWords, worldWords } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { chanBootBind, chanRegWords } from "../../../runtime/chan"
import { schedWords } from "../../../runtime/sched"
import { stwPollOn, stwScanCount, stwUnheldCount, stwOwnScanCount } from "../../../runtime/stw"

// Sized exactly as _tests_/stress/stwcollect's own private collector — this
// program needs no more than one live object and a handful of throwaway
// ones, but there is no reason to under-provision a proven arena size.
const ARENA_WORDS: int = 131072
const INDEX_WORDS: int = 8192
const MARK_STACK: int = 64
const MIN_TRIGGER: int = 4096
const GROWTH_PCT: int = 200
const CHAN_RESERVE_WORDS: int = 4096

// One leaf TypeInfo: a 16-byte body with no pointer fields, so the tracer
// never reinterprets its contents. `MI_REFARR`'s row is never written; only
// its ADDRESS is used, as the `ref_array_info` sentinel `gcInit` matches by
// identity, not by contents.
const META_WORDS: int = 14
const MI_NODE: int = 0
const MI_REFARR: int = 7

// `SafepointFrame` (ABI.md §4): ret, fp, then 32 register words. `ret = 0`
// makes the precise walk of THIS thread's own frame a deliberate no-op — a
// raw `int` is never in the emitted stack map regardless, and the classes
// this file is about are the conservative ones, not the precise one.
const SNAP_WORDS: int = 34

// Bounded garbage-allocation count after the forced collection: enough that a
// swept block is likely reused (strengthening the readback below) without
// costing more than a handful of iterations.
const CHURN_N: int = 8

// A payload with no plausible accidental match: not 0, not a small index, not
// anything the churn loop below writes.
const PAYLOAD: int = 987654321

const JOIN_NS: int = 10000000000        // 10s

// Bundles the three `stwPollOn` addresses that never change across this
// thread's whole run, purely to keep `stwChildCollectAndCheck` under this
// repo's 5-param limit (E0202).
class PollAddrs {
  sched: int,
  reg: int,
  world: int,
}

let doneWord: [1]i32
let okResult: [1]i32
let valResult: [1]i64
let heldResult: [1]i64
let sleepScratch: [2]i64

fn sleepAdapt(ns: int) {
  parkSleepNs(ns, ptrOf(sleepScratch))
}

// The body run on a raw OS thread this program creates directly. NOT
// `@nosplit` — it allocates, ordinarily, and reaches real safepoints on its
// own loop back-edges, exactly as _tests_/stress/thread/gcprops's `gcChild`
// does. `arg` carries nothing this body needs.
fn stwChild(arg: *byte): *byte {
  // The private collector, sized and wired exactly as
  // _tests_/stress/stwcollect's is. `schedBlock` is left all-zero on purpose:
  // no task is ever registered in it, so this thread's own collection can
  // never find one whose mapping contains its `sp`.
  let arena = []i64(ARENA_WORDS)
  let indexArena = []i64(INDEX_WORDS)
  let heapBlock = []i64(heapWords)
  let heapLock = []i32(1)
  let state = []i64(gcWords)
  let meta = []i64(META_WORDS)
  let worldBlock = []i64(worldWords)
  let schedBlock = []i64(schedWords)
  let chanReserve = []i64(CHAN_RESERVE_WORDS)
  let reserveDesc = []i64(reserveWords)
  let regBlock = []i64(chanRegWords)
  let snapBuf = []i64(SNAP_WORDS)

  let metaAddr = int(ptrOf(meta))
  meta[MI_NODE + 0] = 16
  meta[MI_NODE + 1] = 0
  meta[MI_NODE + 2] = 0
  let nodeInfo = metaAddr + MI_NODE * 8
  let refArrInfo = metaAddr + MI_REFARR * 8

  let hp = ptrOf(heapBlock)
  heapInit(hp)
  let hlk = ptrOf(heapLock)
  atomicStore(hlk, spinUnlocked)

  let g = ptrOf(state)
  gcBindMemory(g, int(ptrOf(arena)), ARENA_WORDS * 8, int(ptrOf(indexArena)), INDEX_WORDS * 8, 4096)
  if (!gcInit(g, int(hp), int(hlk), int(hlk), refArrInfo, MARK_STACK)) {
    atomicStore(ptrOf(okResult), i32(0))
    atomicStore(ptrOf(valResult), i64(0))
    atomicStore(ptrOf(doneWord), i32(0))
    return nil
  }
  // Force-collect on every poll (the same "precise-rooting oracle" config
  // _tests_/stress/stwcollect uses), so the exposure below is deterministic
  // regardless of this binary's ambient BIT_GC policy.
  gcConfigure(g, true, true, MIN_TRIGGER, GROWTH_PCT, false)

  chanBootBind(int(ptrOf(reserveDesc)), int(ptrOf(chanReserve)), CHAN_RESERVE_WORDS * 8,
               int(ptrOf(regBlock)), int(ptrOf(schedBlock)))

  let snap = int(ptrOf(snapBuf))
  let addrs = PollAddrs{ sched: int(ptrOf(schedBlock)), reg: int(ptrOf(regBlock)), world: int(ptrOf(worldBlock)) }
  stwChildCollectAndCheck(g, addrs, snap, nodeInfo)

  // Hold every backing slice to this point: only raw, untraced addresses
  // (`g`, `raw`, `snap`, ...) are used above, so the LIVE ambient collector —
  // which tracks these ordinary slices normally — must not be allowed to
  // reclaim any of them out from under the private one while it still reads
  // their bytes. Same technique _tests_/stress/stwcollect uses, via an atomic
  // store instead of a print: a raw OS thread created through
  // runtime/thread/darwin must not use `!`/`fail`/`?` (no per-thread error
  // slot on Darwin outside libSystem's own TLS), and this project's own
  // `print` cannot be assumed clear of that.
  let heldWords = len(arena) + len(indexArena) + len(heapBlock) + len(heapLock) + len(state) +
    len(meta) + len(worldBlock) + len(schedBlock) + len(chanReserve) + len(reserveDesc) +
    len(regBlock) + len(snapBuf)
  atomicStore(ptrOf(heldResult), i64(heldWords))

  atomicStore(ptrOf(doneWord), i32(0))
  return nil
}

// The actual root-class-under-test logic, split out of `stwChild` purely to
// stay under this repo's 80-line function limit — every argument here is a
// raw `int` address into blocks `stwChild` still owns and holds alive, so the
// split changes nothing about which stack frame anything lives on.
fn stwChildCollectAndCheck(g: *i64, addrs: PollAddrs, snap: int, nodeInfo: int) {
  // `ret = 0` in `snap` makes the precise walk of this thread's own frame a
  // deliberate no-op — a raw `int` is never in the emitted stack map
  // regardless, and the classes this file is about are the conservative
  // ones, not the precise one.
  storeWord(snap + 0, 0)
  storeWord(snap + 8, 0)

  // ROOT CLASS UNDER TEST (#2645, epic #1837): the object's address lives
  // ONLY in `raw`, a local of type `int`. Never converted to, or stored as, a
  // typed reference anywhere on this thread.
  let raw: int = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
  storeWord(raw, PAYLOAD)

  // Force the collection from THIS thread, then churn a bounded number of
  // throwaway objects so a swept block is likely reused rather than merely
  // left unmarked.
  let i = 0
  while (i < CHURN_N) {
    stwPollOn(int(g), addrs.sched, addrs.reg, addrs.world, snap)
    let junk = gcAlloc(g, nodeInfo)
    storeWord(junk, i)
    i = i + 1
  }

  let owned = gcOwns(g, raw)
  let got = loadWord(raw)
  if (owned && got == PAYLOAD) {
    atomicStore(ptrOf(okResult), i32(1))
  } else {
    atomicStore(ptrOf(okResult), i32(0))
  }
  atomicStore(ptrOf(valResult), i64(got))
}

fn stwChildEntry(): *byte {
  return entryOf(stwChild)
}

fn main() {
  atomicStore(ptrOf(doneWord), i32(threadRunning))
  let handle = threadStart(stwChildEntry(), byteAt(0), ptrOf(doneWord))
  if (handle == 0) {
    print("FAIL start\n")
    return
  }
  let joined = threadJoin(ptrOf(doneWord), JOIN_NS, sleepAdapt, parkMonoNs)
  if (joined != threadJoinDone) {
    print("FAIL join\n")
    return
  }

  let ok = atomicLoad(ptrOf(okResult)) != 0
  let val = atomicLoad(ptrOf(valResult))
  if (ok) {
    print("PASS\n")
  } else {
    print("FAIL observed=${val}\n")
  }

  // Process-wide, incremented by `stwScanTasks` on every call this run made —
  // including the one forced above, from a thread no scheduler task owns.
  let scans = stwScanCount()
  let unheld = stwUnheldCount()
  // `stwOwnScanCount` (#2651) is the subset of `unheld` in which
  // `stwScanOwnStack` actually scanned a range, not merely attempted one —
  // see stw.bit's header comment above `stwScans`. The two increment in the
  // same guard in `stwScanTasks`, so `unheld` is exactly the number of
  // ATTEMPTS at class 8b; `ownScans` is whether each attempt actually found
  // a published, containing bound to scan. They must read equal on this
  // thread's own private collector, whose stack top/base are published by
  // the very first `stwPollOn` call before any collection it forces can run
  // (`worldEnsureSelfOn` is the first line of `stwPollOn`). Printed as raw
  // counts, not derived booleans, ONLY on the mismatch branch below — raw
  // counts are not reproducible across `BIT_GC` policies (see the module
  // comment above), but a mismatch is itself the failure and this program
  // never reaches `.expected` byte-comparison on that path.
  let ownScans = stwOwnScanCount()
  if (unheld != ownScans) {
    print("FAIL ownscan mismatch unheld=${unheld} ownScans=${ownScans}\n")
  } else {
    print("scans>0=${scans > 0} unheld>0=${unheld > 0} unheld==ownScans=${unheld == ownScans}\n")
  }

  let _ = threadRelease(handle)
}
